## Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with uniform but broad tunability of pore size, large surface area, and tailorable chemistry[1-3] have met the requirements for gas storage/separation,[4,5] sensing,[6,7] catalysis[8,9] and other applications. Although the last two decades have witnessed increasing researches in these fields built around MOFs, the frameworks of MOFs sometimes show low stability under external chemical or mechanical conditions due to their relatively labile coordination bonds, which might be the main obstacle to the industrial advancement of MOFs.[10-12] To favor the development of MOFs, improving stability of frameworks has become a main focus. Approaches such as modulated synthesis[13] (e.g., adding competitive modulars to slow down nucleation process) and postmodification treatment[14] (e.g., covering MOFs with hydrophobic protector) that showed improved environment endurance and enhanced applied performance have been proposed. More interestingly, some researchers have cast their eyes on converting unstable MOFs into their analogies or derivatives for extended applications, with crystal structure and chemical composition transformation being exemplary methods.[15-19] Studies on these conversion processes of MOFs showed two main reaction patterns: in-situ transformation[20] and dissolution-regeneration,[21] and both of them provided the evidence that metal ions in MOFs can diffuse under certain conditions. According to this finding and to make full use of the instability of MOFs, we preliminarily think that MOFs have the potential to be used as metal ion precursors for releasing metal ions rather than as self-sacrificial templates only.
Metal ions in MOFs were further evaluated to investigate the feasibility of MOFs as metal ion precursors. To date, various metal ions have been reported in synthesized MOFs, including alkali metal (e.g., K, Mg and Al), rare-earth metal (e.g., Eu, Hf and Ce) and transition metal (e.g., Fe, Co and Cu). The abundance of metal ions enables MOFs to be selected as a large database for specific applications.[22,23] Besides, the periodically connected metal nodes and organic ligands in 3D crystal framework indicate the homogeneous dispersion of metal ions, which should be beneficial to achieving continuous releasing of metal ions in certain reaction conditions. Moreover, with appropriate chemical treatments in liquid phase systems, metal ions in MOFs can be transformed into metal compounds such as metal oxides, sulfides, and phosphides.[24-28] For example, Zhang’s group reported a 2D Cu-TCPP (tetrakis (4- carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) nanosheet and turned some of the Cu sites into CuS by direct sulfidation in ethanol solution.[29] Through a two-step conversion reaction, Lou’s group obtained double-shelled cobalt hydroxide@NiCo layered double hydroxide (LDH) nanocages from ZIF-67.[30] Hence, metal ions in MOFs with vast choice, homogeneous distribution and the ability to prepare metal compounds, together with their inner week anchor on frameworks by coordination bonds, make us deduce that MOFs are promising candidates for realizing control release of metal ions under suitable conditions, which is expected to exert meaningful influence on nanosynthesis (e.g., size regulation and morphology control) in liquid phase systems.[31-33]
Here, we report a concept that MOFs can be used as metal ion precursors for the construction of various functional nanocomposites in liquid phase synthetic systems (Figure 1). Our strategy rationally takes advantages of the instability of MOFs and the well-dispersion of metal ions. With this strategy, transition metal sulfides (TMSs) and LDHs were grown on substrates like carbon nanotubes (CNTs), graphene oxide (GO), silica $(\mathsf{S i O}_{2})$ , etc. It is worth noting that the obtained functional nanocomposites not only maintained the structures and properties of substrates but also formed a homogeneous coating layer themselves. In contrast, the well-defined structure could not be easily prepared by using metal salts or metal oxides as metal ion precursors. As a result, the representative synthesized CoS with CNTs as substrates showed enhanced electrochemical performance due to the optimized structural synergy and the contribution of CNTs substrates as effective conductive networks. In addition, given the rapid development of nanomaterials, many MOFs and substrates are available to be taken advantage of and thus it is highly possible to endow the strategy with more flexibility and designability.
