## Results and Discussion
Heterogeneous growth of TMS on CNTs. MOFs as metal ion precursors were demonstrated initially by synthesizing TMS on CNTs. CNTs were chosen as substrates for their good electrical properties, stability and wide applications.[34] Taking zeolite imidazole framework-67 (ZIF-67) as an example, in a typical one-pot experiment, CNTs were first modified with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) and then mixed with the assynthesized ZIF-67 (Figures 2a and S1a). The mixture was further sulfidated in ethanol solution containing thioacetamide (TAA) at $110^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 3 h. Finally, CNTs were covered by homogenous shells of CoS nanoparticles and the uniformly core-shell structured composites were formed (the optimization of the morphology was shown in Figure S2), denoted as CNT@CoS (Figure 1b). The shell thickness of CoS was averagely around 9 nm (Figure S1b) and, the homogeneous dispersion of S and Co elements on CNTs was observed by scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) elemental mappings (Figure 2c). The main peaks in X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern (Figure S1c) referred to indexed (100), (101), (102) and (110) planes of CoS (JCPDS No. 65-8977). The weight content of CNTs was calculated to be about $32.4\%$ by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) method (Figure S3).
This strategy was also expanded to other MOFs with modified synthetic conditions. ZIF-8, another MOF materials with a zeolite imidazole structure (Figures 2d and S4a) containing zinc ions was used to synthesize ZnS on CNTs. The homogeneous $\mathsf{C N T@Z n S}$ structure was verified by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image (Figure 2e) and STEM elemental mappings (Figure 2f). Carboxylate-based ligands contained MOFs like HKUST-1 (Cu) (Figures 2g and S4b) and MIL-88 (Fe) (Figures 2j and S4c) were utilized for the synthesis of CNT@CuS (Figures 2h and 2i) and $\mathsf{C N T@F e S}_{2}$ (Figures 2k and 2l). The related structure characterizations of above nanocomposites were shown in XRD patterns (Figures S4d-f).
These results preliminarily confirmed that MOFs can be used as metal ion precursors.
Heterogeneous growth process of CNT@CoS and mechanism study. Two major reasons might be accountable for realizing MOFs as metal ion precursors: i) homogeneously distributed metal ions in MOFs can be released in mild reaction conditions due to relatively weak coordination bonds; ii) slow feeding of metal ions leads to continuous growth process which is beneficial to improving the morphology of nanostructure. Based on the above hypotheses, the detailed reaction process of ZIF-67 as metal ion precursor was further studied. Timedependent TEM was firstly investigated to monitor the growth process. Within 1 h, Co ions were released from ZIF-67 and coordinated with $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ hydrolyzed from TAA, and then amorphous CoS layers were seen to uniformly cover the surface of CNTs (Figures 3a and 3b). With the prolonging of time, the amorphous CoS gently transformed into crystal ones during heating process and the CNTs were densely and homogeneously covered by CoS nanoparticles (Figure 3c).
The heterogeneous growth process was further verified by the strong interaction between CNTs and CoS to exclude a week-interacted deposition process. First, to analyze the elemental states, ZIF-67 derived CoS (Figure S5) was prepared and tested by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure 3d). The Co 2p3/2 spectrum showed four curve-fitted peaks. Two intensive peaks at 778.9 eV and 780.6 eV suggested the formation of Co-S bond, while peaks at $782.5~\mathsf{e V}$ and 786.1 eV corresponding to surface Co-O and Co-OH bonds might be derived from the affinity of Co for atmospheric oxygen.[35,36] However, in the XPS result of $\mathsf{C N T@C o S}$ (Figures 3d and S6), the Co 2p peaks shifted positively to higher binding energy $(\Delta=$ $0.2\ {\mathsf{e V}})$ than CoS corresponding to the migration of electron cloud induced by electronegative CNTs, indicating the interaction between CoS and CNTs.[37] Next, Raman spectrum (Figure 3e) was characterized in which the R value $(\mathsf{R}=\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{D}}/\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{G}})$ is used to evaluate the degree of graphitization of CNTs (D band at $1,359~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ , G band at $1,581~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1},$ ). The obviously increased
R value from 0.996 of CNTs to 1.005 of CNT@CoS indicated that more structural defects were originated from the growth of CoS on CNTs via chemical bonds.[38] The $\mathsf{x p s}$ and Raman results strongly reflected the chemical interaction between CoS and CNTs, and the heterogeneous growth nature of the reaction process.
Time-dependent inductively coupled plasma (ICP) measurement was employed for detecting the concentration of cobalt ions. As can be seen from the time-concentration plots (Figure 3f), the concentration of Co ions except that at the beginning remained at an average low value $(0.07~\mathsf{m M})$ during the whole reaction process, indicating the slow and continuous release of Co ions. To compare the advantages of taking MOFs as precursors, cobalt nitrate with different concentrations were used in control experiments (Table S1). First, cobalt nitrate with cobalt ion concentration of $5~\mathsf{m M}$ (calculated according to the total amount of cobalt element in ZIF-67) was intentionally introduced. However, only cobalt sulfides with large particle size were decorated on the surface of CNTs, accompanied by many aggregated cobalt sulfide clusters in solutions (Figures S7a and S7b). With the decrease in concentrations of cobalt nitrate, the amount of aggregated cobalt sulfide clusters became fewer whereas cobalt sulfides decorated on CNTs was still uneven. In brief, by adjusting the amount of cobalt nitrate, neither the higher nor lower concentration could we get the desired morphology as that using ZIF-67 as cobalt ion precursor. Some related works also showed that the homogeneous deposition of cobalt sulfides on CNTs was hardly achievable by using cobalt salts (e.g., $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{A c}})_{2})$ as precursors.[39-43] Additionally, $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were taken as another kind of metal source for comparison. However, it showed no reaction behaviour under the same conditions, as verified by both TEM images and XRD pattern (Figures S7c-f), indicating the strong covalent bond in $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ was not ideal for ion release under this condition. These main four growth patterns were illustrated in Figure 3g.
Based on the above results, some assumptions were proposed to explain the growth process. First, the substrate interface provided with residual valence force can reduce the surface energy of nucleation, and the Gibbs free energy of heterogeneous nucleation is lower than that of homogeneous nucleation.[44] Second, theoretically, homogeneous nucleation and heterogeneous nucleation have the same critical radius. Under the conditions of low ion concentration, heterogeneous nucleation is favorable due to the requirement of fewer amount of monomer. With ZIF-67 being the precursor, the control release of cobalt ions will lead to low concentration and CNTs surface with chemical sites can endow cobalt sulfide with reduced nucleation energy, thus facilitating the heterogeneous growth process.
Heterogeneous growth of CNT@LDH and extension of substrates. Inspired by the synthesis of CNT@CoS, we extended this strategy to build CNT@LDH hybrid materials. Briefly, the mixture of PVP-modified CNTs and ZIF-67 was dispersed into ethanol and then mixed with nickel nitrate under stirring and reacted under $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $4\ h$ . The final core-shell structure was shown in TEM images with CNTs as the core and LDH as the shell (Figure S8a). Characteristic peaks of CNT@LDH in XRD pattern (Figure S8b) matched well with those of hydrotalcite (JSPDS NO. 38-0715). The uniform distribution of Co, Ni and O elements on CNTs surface was observed by STEM elemental mappings (Figure S8c). The molar ratio of Co:Ni calculated about 1:2 was tested by energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) analysis (Figure S8d).
Our strategy also allowed the fabrication of nanomaterials on other substrates. Silica $(\mathsf{S i O}_{2})$ , ferroferric oxide $(F e_{3}O_{4})$ , UiO-66-NH2 and graphene oxide (GO) (Figures 4a-d) were further studied as exemplary substrates under the same reaction conditions. Some pre-treatments of substrates are required owing to their low affinity to metal ions. For example, $\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ should be modified by surfactants like PVP to obtain more homogeneous structure (Figure S9). As for the $-N H_{2}$ contained UiO-66-NH2 and $-{\mathsf{O H//-C O O H}}$ contained GO, they possess good affinity that promises them to be used as substrates directly. The corresponding TMSs (Figures 4e-h) and LDHs (Figures 4i-l) can be formed on different substrates with homogeneous morphology. STEM mapping images (Figure S10) and XRD patterns (Figure S11) showed the uniform distribution of elements and well-maintained crystallinity, respectively.
Electrochemical behavior of CNT@CoS composites. Cobalt sulfides with high energy density have been widely studied in batteries, but they often suffer from fast capacity fading.[45-47] In our work, the as-prepared CNT@CoS composite revealed a well-designed structure that was conducive to improving capacity retention, and thus was used as anode material in LIBs (Figure 5a).
Galvanostatic charge–discharge curves of the first three cycles of CNT@CoS were measured at $100\ m{\mathsf{A h\ 9}}^{-1}$ between 0.05 and 3 V (Figure 5b). During the first cathodic scan, inclined plateaus around 1.3 and $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ should be assigned to the reduction of CoS and the formation of SEI (solid electrolyte interphase) film, respectively.[48] After the first cycle, the anodic sweep with the same plateau positions as the cathodic sweep and the nearly overlapped charge/discharge curves indicated that CNT@CoS had good reversibility. This total trend was also consistent with a typical cyclic voltammogram (CV) test (Figure S12a). The rate capability of CNT@CoS electrode was explored with gradually increased current densities (Figure 5c). When measured at 100, 200 and $500\ \mathrm{\mA\}\ \mathfrak{g}^{-1}$ , the average discharge capacities delivered were 790, 701 and 602 mAh ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. A decent capacity of 484 mAh ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ was still maintained even at a high current density of $1,000\ \mathsf{m A}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Finally, an elevated capacity of $836\ m{\tt A h}\ {\tt g}^{-1}$ can be recovered when current density reversed back to $100\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the capacity showed bare loss after 200 cycles with high Coulombic efficiency at $500\ \mathsf{m A}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figures S12b and S13). The above performance was comparable to recent works (Table S2). Such good electrode performance might be ascribed to the following improvements: first, the ultrafine CoS nanoparticles shortened the Li-ion diffusion distance; second, conductive CNTs core enabled fast electron transfer; third, CNTs skeleton strengthened the mechanical stress ability of the composites, therefore improving total cycle stability.
For further investigation, the cycling performance of different anode materials were tested at current density of 500 mA ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure 5d). Compared to the quick fading of ZIF-67 derived CoS after 30 cycles and the relative low energy capacity of bare CNTs, CNT@CoS demonstrated a stable cycling performance resulting from the synergistic effect between CNTs and CoS. To deepen the understanding of kinetic properties, electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) was tested at an open-circuit voltage (3.0 V) (Figure S14). All the plots in picture were composed of high frequency semicircles and low frequency sloping lines which referred to the charge transfer resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}})$ at electrolyte interface and the Li-ion transform resistance in electrode, respectively.[44] The CNT@CoS electrode showed smaller $\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{c t}}$ value compared with CoS in both initial (179.2 and 344.9 Ω) and cycled (46.2 and $83.6~\Omega\$ ) cells, indicating lower charge transform barrier at the interface and better conductivity. In addition, when compared with CNT/CoS (synthesized by using cobalt nitrate as cobalt precursor), CNT@CoS showed higher energy density due to the uniform and dense growth of CoS on CNTs.
## Conclusion
Our concept of taking MOFs as metal ion precursors to construct functional nanocomposites is dependent on three factors. One is the variety of metal ions in different MOFs. In this case, MOFs serve as a large database to be selected for releasing metal ions. For example, transition metals and their compounds can be applied to energy storage and conversion, offering effective solutions for today’s energy crisis. The second factor goes to the unique structure of MOFs. MOFs process uniformly distributed metal ions that are connected with ligands by coordination bonds. These relatively week chemical bonds can be broken and lead to a continuous release in appropriate conditions. The slow feeding of ions can modulate the thermodynamics and kinetics in whole reaction system, and thus the uniform and unique nanostructure will form. The third factor is heterogeneous growth ability on different substrates. Substrates play important roles in functional composites. For example, the conductivity and adsorption capacity can be imparted by choosing CNTs or graphene as substrates; the biocompatibility can be given by using biologic materials (e.g., antibacterial material, cellulose) as substrates.
In summary, we have demonstrated a general concept for constructing nanomaterials on substrates to form functional composites by using MOFs as metal ion precursors in liquid phase systems. Time-dependent tests and control experiments reveal a continuous and homogenous nature of the releasing process. Synthesized CNT@CoS shows improved performance in LIBs, declaring the preponderances of our structure design that combines active centres with functional substrates. Furthermore, this strategy is extendable to various MOFs and substrates to prepare varied composites with novel architectures. In addition, we believe that our concept can not only widen the applications of MOFs for being explored as solid-state metal ion precursors, but also provide a platform for studies on structure-function relationship and interface interaction.
